  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-8$ and the leading coefficient is $10.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of\u00a0}-8}{\\text{factor of\u00a0}10}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-\\frac{6}{5}=\\frac{-6}{5}$ does not take the above form. The numerator $(-6)$ is not a factor of $-8.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
